# Check every text file out with LF on every platform.
#
# Windows runners default to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites the working tree
# to CRLF at checkout. Every text path in this repository is stored LF in the
# index today, so `eol=lf` renormalizes nothing -- it only stops a Windows
# checkout from disagreeing with the bytes that were committed.
#
# visp-intel carries this same line for the same reason: its schema trust root is
# a SHA-256 over committed text, and a CRLF checkout made the same commit compute
# a different hash on Windows than on Linux.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The vendored conformance vectors are pinned by content, not by commit id:
# `manifest.json` records a byte length and a SHA-256 for every document beside
# it, and `test_vendored_vectors_match_their_manifest` verifies both on every
# run. Memory does not regenerate these bytes, it conforms to them, so any
# end-of-line translation is not cosmetic here -- it is the difference between
# the recorded bytes and different bytes.
#
# `-text` rather than `binary` so the JSON still renders in a diff.
#
# Measured: under a CRLF checkout each file grows by exactly its newline count
# (+1, +1, +19, +1, +1), and `expected-file-grain.json` -- first in the test's
# sorted order -- goes 8449 -> 8450, which is the assertion that failed on
# windows-latest.
tests/vectors/** -text
